# A shell-command fixer re-executes the previous command it reads from the
# SH_PREV_CMD environment variable: src/main.rs `env::var("SH_PREV_CMD")`
# -> `misc::expand_aliases` -> `fix::fix_command` -> `get_command_output`
# -> `shell_words::split` -> `Command::new(&split_command[0]).args(..)`.
# Re-running the command is the tool's documented purpose; the defect is
# what the child inherits. At the parent (5293957b^, 1979ca4) nothing on
# that path examines or drops the process's own credentials, so with the
# binary setuid/setgid, or launched under sudo or doas, the re-executed
# command runs with the elevated effective uid/gid and keeps SUDO_UID,
# SUDO_GID, SUDO_USER, SUDO_COMMAND and DOAS_USER in its environment --
# a caller-controlled command executed as root (CWE-269; the advisory
# branch is advisory-fix/command-reexec).
#
# The fixed revision 5293957b adds PermissionIssue::detect/fix in the new
# src/fix/output.rs. detect() distinguishes euid != getuid(), SUDO_UID/
# SUDO_GID set, and DOAS_USER set; fix() installs a `pre_exec` hook calling
# libc::initgroups/setgid/setuid to return to the invoking user before the
# child execs, rewrites USER/HOME, and env_remove()s the five sudo/doas
# variables. The command executed, its argv and the variable it comes from
# are byte-identical across the revision pair; only credential handling
# changed.
#
# Measured, both revisions, three --cache off runs each over the whole
# checkout: 3 findings at the parent and 6 at the fixed revision, every one VYQL-MEM-006
# NullableDeref review noise (the fixed revision's three extra rows are that rule on the
# new output.rs). Nothing touches the re-execution path, the credential
# handling, or either revision's std::process::Command.
#
# The taint route is not the discriminator, and that is measured rather
# than assumed. Rust binds both ends (envVariable0 on `env.var`,
# commandExecution11/13 on `Command::new`/`.args`) and VYQL-INJ-002 fires on
# this shape -- case 1 below. To check whether the route separates the
# revisions, both checkouts are rescanned with the module-qualified hops
# rewritten as use-imported bare calls, which is the only change: the
# parent then reports CRITICAL VYQL-INJ-002 from SH_PREV_CMD and so does
# the fixed revision, identically. That finding is not the re-execution sink, though --
# on both revisions its sink is `structs::Command::new(command, ..)` at
# src/fix.rs:36/:56, the application's own `pub struct Command { command,
# output: CommandOutput }` data constructor, which commandExecution11's
# `callee.path ~= "Command.new"` matches because `~=` is segment-aware in
# both directions (the urlFetch123 defect family). The real
# std::process::Command spawn at get_command_output is reached by neither
# revision, through any spelling, for the two stacked reasons cases 2-4
# isolate.
#
# What would separate the revisions is the credential axis, and the
# vulnerable revision carries no code fact for it: whether the process is
# privileged is a deployment property the fixed revision itself only learns at runtime
# from geteuid()/getuid() and the sudo/doas environment. The one code-level
# proxy -- "spawns a Command with no pre_exec/setuid/setgid" -- is
# unshippable and measured: 354 `Command::new` call sites in 204 .rs files
# across the corpus clones, of which exactly one file contains pre_exec or
# setuid. A 354-for-1 key, against the 51-for-1 shape recorded as
# measured-unshippable.
#
# Reuse is checked before concluding. code.CommandStringWrapperExecution
# and code.DynamicCommandExec are the CWE-78 assembly axis -- a command
# string built before execution; this path is argv-split and the assembly is
# the tool's function. code.ParsedUserIdDefaultRootPrivilegeEscalation is
# CWE-269 but the UID-parsing-default axis; PrivilegedWorkflowUntrusted-
# CodeExecution, PrivilegedContainer and PrivilegedOp are CI-workflow, IaC
# and smartcontract axes. A sibling concept for "re-executes an untrusted
# command while holding elevated credentials" is emittable only through the
# 354-for-1 absence key, so it would be a concept with no honest binding.
# The 1380 counterweight fails in both directions: the fixed revision adds the
# documented calls (geteuid, initgroups, setuid) rather than leaving one
# behind, and keying on their absence flags every subprocess spawn.
#
# ATTENTION, the privilege-state/configuration family (1514, 1535, 1540,
# 1556, 1557, 1593): the discriminator is runtime credential state that no
# static fact in the vulnerable revision expresses.
#
# Two engine gaps stack on the real path, and the cases below ladder through
# them one construct at a time.
#
# (a) Rust function returns carry no taint -- the gap on file at rank 1632
#     (rust.go emits no nir.Return, so `expand_aliases`' result is clean at
#     its call site). Case 1 -> case 2 isolates it inside a single file.
# (b) A Rust module-qualified free-function call never resolves. rust.go's
#     expr() flattens a scoped_identifier into one nir.Name whose ID keeps
#     the `::` source spelling, while resolveTargets keys funcQual by
#     `<file-module>::<bare name>` and funcShort by the bare name alone, so
#     `fix::fix_command` misses both and the guarded unique-name fallback is
#     never consulted with the right key. The same callee reached through
#     `use fix::fix_command;` and a bare call does resolve. This is not the
#     1364 family (delegated `self`-method calls inside an impl); it is a
#     free function reached through its module path, and it is the idiomatic
#     spelling -- 1286 of the 9721 .rs files in the corpus clones declare a
#     module and call through it. Case 3 -> case 4 isolates it.
#
# When either gap closes, the cases pinning it must be revisited: case 2
# flips to expect when returns propagate, and case 4 when module-qualified
# calls resolve. Case 5 stays reject against a credential rule, which does
# not exist today.

test "rank1847: environment-supplied command re-executed -- the route itself works"
  lang rust
  file src/main.rs
  code
  ```
  use std::process::Command;

  pub fn fix_command(command: String, expand_command: String) -> std::io::Result<String> {
      let _ = command;
      let command_output = get_command_output(expand_command)?;
      Ok(command_output.stdout)
  }

  fn get_command_output(expand_command: String) -> std::io::Result<CommandOutput> {
      let split_command = shell_words::split(&expand_command)
          .map_err(|e| std::io::Error::other(format!("Failed to parse command: {e}")))?;

      if split_command.is_empty() {
          return Err(std::io::Error::other("Empty command provided"));
      }

      let child = Command::new(&split_command[0])
          .args(&split_command[1..])
          .env("LANG", "C")
          .env("LC_ALL", "C")
          .spawn()?;

      let output = child.wait_with_output()?;
      Ok(CommandOutput::from(output))
  }

  fn main() -> Result<(), std::io::Error> {
      let command =
          std::env::var("SH_PREV_CMD").context("SH_PREV_CMD environment variable is not set.")?;
      // the expand_aliases hop is elided here because a Rust function's
      // return value carries no taint (rank 1632); the environment value is
      // passed as the expand_command argument directly so the route under
      // test is the one the tool really takes from fix_command onwards.
      let fixed_command = fix_command(command.clone(), command)?;
      println!("{fixed_command}");
      Ok(())
  }
  ```
  expect VYQL-INJ-002

test "rank1847: the same value crossing one function return loses its taint"
  lang rust
  file src/main.rs
  code
  ```
  use std::process::Command;

  fn expand_aliases(command: &str) -> String {
      command.to_string()
  }

  pub fn fix_command(command: String, expand_command: String) -> std::io::Result<String> {
      let _ = command;
      let command_output = get_command_output(expand_command)?;
      Ok(command_output.stdout)
  }

  fn get_command_output(expand_command: String) -> std::io::Result<CommandOutput> {
      let split_command = shell_words::split(&expand_command)
          .map_err(|e| std::io::Error::other(format!("Failed to parse command: {e}")))?;

      if split_command.is_empty() {
          return Err(std::io::Error::other("Empty command provided"));
      }

      let child = Command::new(&split_command[0])
          .args(&split_command[1..])
          .env("LANG", "C")
          .env("LC_ALL", "C")
          .spawn()?;

      let output = child.wait_with_output()?;
      Ok(CommandOutput::from(output))
  }

  fn main() -> Result<(), std::io::Error> {
      let command =
          std::env::var("SH_PREV_CMD").context("SH_PREV_CMD environment variable is not set.")?;
      let expand_command = expand_aliases(&command);
      let fixed_command = fix_command(command, expand_command)?;
      println!("{fixed_command}");
      Ok(())
  }
  ```
  reject VYQL-INJ-002

